1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,754
Aarav taps the button on his desk lamp.

2
00:00:02,754 --> 00:00:03,654
It turns on.

3
00:00:03,940 --> 00:00:06,303
He lets go, and the lamp stays on.

4
00:00:06,303 --> 00:00:08,320
Another tap, and it goes off.

5
00:00:08,620 --> 00:00:09,674
The button forgets.

6
00:00:09,674 --> 00:00:10,728
The lamp remembers.

7
00:00:10,728 --> 00:00:12,094
What does the remembering?

8
00:00:12,594 --> 00:00:15,152
The gates we have met
so far have no memory.

9
00:00:15,502 --> 00:00:18,637
Change the input, and the
output changes at once.

10
00:00:18,987 --> 00:00:20,562
A flip-flop is different.

11
00:00:20,562 --> 00:00:23,023
It stores one bit, a zero or a one.

12
00:00:23,373 --> 00:00:26,854
And it keeps that bit, even
after the input goes away.

13
00:00:27,204 --> 00:00:29,358
It has two outputs, Q and Q bar.

14
00:00:29,358 --> 00:00:30,900
They are always opposite.

15
00:00:31,550 --> 00:00:33,879
The simplest is the SR flip-flop.

16
00:00:33,879 --> 00:00:34,913
S means set.

17
00:00:34,913 --> 00:00:36,048
R means reset.

18
00:00:36,398 --> 00:00:37,746
Take two NOR gates.

19
00:00:37,746 --> 00:00:40,395
Feed each output back into the other gate.

20
00:00:40,745 --> 00:00:42,378
That loop is the memory.

21
00:00:42,378 --> 00:00:44,636
Each gate holds the other in place.

22
00:00:45,286 --> 00:00:46,186
Press set.

23
00:00:46,067 --> 00:00:46,967
S is one.

24
00:00:46,857 --> 00:00:47,872
Q becomes one.

25
00:00:48,222 --> 00:00:49,122
Let go.

26
00:00:48,936 --> 00:00:50,215
S is zero again.

27
00:00:50,215 --> 00:00:51,494
But Q stays one.

28
00:00:51,494 --> 00:00:52,505
It remembers.

29
00:00:52,855 --> 00:00:53,779
Press reset.

30
00:00:53,779 --> 00:00:54,679
R is one.

31
00:00:54,618 --> 00:00:55,742
Q becomes zero.

32
00:00:56,092 --> 00:00:56,992
Let go.

33
00:00:56,816 --> 00:00:57,903
Q stays zero.

34
00:00:58,253 --> 00:01:01,047
So S and R both zero means no change.

35
00:01:01,047 --> 00:01:02,899
It holds the last value.

36
00:01:03,549 --> 00:01:05,973
But S and R both one is not allowed.

37
00:01:05,973 --> 00:01:07,612
Both outputs go to zero.

38
00:01:07,962 --> 00:01:10,679
Then Q and Q bar are no longer opposite.

39
00:01:10,679 --> 00:01:12,481
This is the invalid state.

40
00:01:13,131 --> 00:01:15,733
Most flip-flops also have a clock input.

41
00:01:15,733 --> 00:01:17,888
It is a steady beat of pulses.

42
00:01:18,238 --> 00:01:21,325
The flip-flop can change only
when a clock pulse arrives.

43
00:01:21,675 --> 00:01:26,015
This keeps many flip-flops working
in step, like dancers on a beat.

44
00:01:26,665 --> 00:01:29,641
The JK flip-flop fixes the invalid state.

45
00:01:29,991 --> 00:01:31,360
J works like set.

46
00:01:31,360 --> 00:01:32,831
K works like reset.

47
00:01:33,181 --> 00:01:34,742
J zero, K zero.

48
00:01:34,742 --> 00:01:35,756
No change.

49
00:01:36,106 --> 00:01:37,356
J one, K zero.

50
00:01:37,356 --> 00:01:38,452
Q becomes one.

51
00:01:38,452 --> 00:01:39,702
J zero, K one.

52
00:01:39,702 --> 00:01:40,846
Q becomes zero.

53
00:01:41,196 --> 00:01:42,642
And J one, K one?

54
00:01:42,642 --> 00:01:44,578
Now Q flips to the opposite.

55
00:01:44,578 --> 00:01:46,111
This is called toggle.

56
00:01:46,761 --> 00:01:49,676
Toggle on every press, and
you get Aarav's lamp button.

57
00:01:50,326 --> 00:01:54,077
The D flip-flop has just
one data input, called D.

58
00:01:54,427 --> 00:01:56,163
Inside, D goes to S.

59
00:01:56,163 --> 00:01:58,405
A NOT gate sends D bar to R.

60
00:01:58,755 --> 00:02:01,274
So S and R can never both be one.

61
00:02:01,274 --> 00:02:03,183
There is no invalid state.

62
00:02:03,833 --> 00:02:05,999
On each clock pulse, Q copies D.

63
00:02:05,999 --> 00:02:07,644
Then it holds that value.

64
00:02:08,294 --> 00:02:11,344
Groups of D flip-flops
store data inside computers.

65
00:02:11,344 --> 00:02:13,035
They are called registers.

66
00:02:13,685 --> 00:02:16,020
A flip-flop is a one bit memory.

67
00:02:16,820 --> 00:02:17,981
Worked example.

68
00:02:17,981 --> 00:02:21,093
AJK flip-flop starts with Q equal to zero.

69
00:02:21,443 --> 00:02:23,332
Four clock pulses arrive.

70
00:02:23,332 --> 00:02:25,123
Find Q after each one.

71
00:02:25,823 --> 00:02:26,723
Pulse one.

72
00:02:26,651 --> 00:02:27,879
J one, K zero.

73
00:02:27,879 --> 00:02:28,859
That is set.

74
00:02:28,859 --> 00:02:29,935
Q becomes one.

75
00:02:30,635 --> 00:02:31,600
Pulse two.

76
00:02:31,600 --> 00:02:32,976
J one, K one.

77
00:02:32,976 --> 00:02:33,876
Toggle.

78
00:02:33,708 --> 00:02:35,139
Q flips to zero.

79
00:02:35,839 --> 00:02:36,899
Pulse three.

80
00:02:36,899 --> 00:02:38,363
J zero, K zero.

81
00:02:38,363 --> 00:02:39,313
No change.

82
00:02:39,313 --> 00:02:40,494
Q stays zero.

83
00:02:41,194 --> 00:02:42,116
Pulse four.

84
00:02:42,116 --> 00:02:43,359
J one, K one.

85
00:02:43,359 --> 00:02:44,381
Toggle again.

86
00:02:44,381 --> 00:02:45,514
Q becomes one.

87
00:02:46,214 --> 00:02:49,117
So Q goes one, zero, zero, one.

88
00:02:49,767 --> 00:02:51,231
Now, the exam trap.

89
00:02:51,581 --> 00:02:55,099
Many students think S and R
both one simply sets Q to one.

90
00:02:55,449 --> 00:02:58,802
In the NOR SR flip-flop,
both outputs go to zero.

91
00:02:59,152 --> 00:03:00,698
Then Q equals Q bar.

92
00:03:00,698 --> 00:03:04,190
That breaks the rule, so
this input is not allowed.

93
00:03:04,840 --> 00:03:06,461
Where is this asked?

94
00:03:06,461 --> 00:03:08,321
In PUC board papers.

95
00:03:08,671 --> 00:03:13,707
Expect the truth tables of SR,
JK and D, and the invalid state.

96
00:03:14,357 --> 00:03:15,259
Try this.

97
00:03:15,259 --> 00:03:18,747
AJK flip-flop has J
and K both tied to one.

98
00:03:18,747 --> 00:03:20,222
Q starts at zero.

99
00:03:20,222 --> 00:03:22,830
What is Q after five clock pulses?

100
00:03:28,230 --> 00:03:29,130
B.

101
00:03:28,631 --> 00:03:29,531
Q is one.

102
00:03:29,842 --> 00:03:33,603
With J and K both one,
every pulse toggles Q.

103
00:03:33,953 --> 00:03:36,846
Q goes one, zero, one, zero, one.

104
00:03:37,196 --> 00:03:39,600
An odd number of toggles flips it.

105
00:03:39,600 --> 00:03:41,021
So Q ends at one.

106
00:03:41,671 --> 00:03:43,217
Screenshot this card.

107
00:03:46,417 --> 00:03:48,710
Next, switches, relays and connectors.

108
00:03:48,710 --> 00:03:51,427
The parts that link
circuits to the real world.
